id: pm-gh1664
title: "GH-1664: pm context --for orient --limit 3 and pm activity --limit 3 reparse every history stream, so a three-row recent-activity read pays archive-scale I/O"
description: "GitHub issue #1664 (reported 2026-11-04 against 2026.11.4 from an agent-UX audit). On a 3,188-item tracker copy with about 124 MB of history in 3,206 streams, the reporter measured p50 16.36 s for pm context --for orient --limit 3, 6.10 s for pm context --limit 3 and 8.21 s for pm activity --limit 3 on a shared, contended host. Source confirms the algorithm: the orient context builds its activity section through runActivity with compact output and a small limit (src/sdk/query/context.ts), and collectActivityEntries in src/sdk/query/activity.ts lists every .jsonl file in the history directory and calls readHistoryEntries on each, so time filtering, sorting and the row limit apply only after the whole archive is parsed. The limit bounds delivery, not history I/O. One uncontended run on this repository's own tracker (126 MB, 3,217 history streams) on 2026-11-05 took 3.27 s for pm context --for orient --limit 3, against 1.48 s for pm context --limit 3 and 1.61 s for pm activity --limit 3, so the activity section roughly doubles the cost of the canonical cold-start read, and that cost grows with history volume rather than item count. Expected: a bounded recent-activity read uses a coherent recent-event index or revision-keyed per-stream summaries, reads only enough verified tails to fill the window and the requested rows, falls back explicitly when the index is unavailable, and keeps history policy and integrity checks, with invalidation on append, replacement, repair, redaction and compaction. Why it matters for agents: every reconnecting agent and parallel worker pays a full-history scan in the canonical cold-start read to obtain three events. Related open work on other axes: pm-801d indexes item files with zero output change, pm-29ymfn owns strict history verification cost, and pm-1wujzi owns the claim-state history fold. The same orient read is the subject of the budget defect pm-gh1662. Duplicate check: searched activity history scan, recent activity index, context latency, history volume scaling and collectActivityEntries across all statuses, and no item owns the activity read's full scan."
type: Issue
status: closed
priority: 2
tags[7]: activity,agent-ux,context,"gh:1664",history,performance,scale
created_at: "2026-11-05T06:09:12.493Z"
updated_at: "2026-11-20T19:44:12.487Z"
closed_at: "2026-11-20T12:31:02.596Z"
completed_at: "2026-11-20T12:31:02.596Z"
author: "harness:claude-code"
estimated_minutes: 480
acceptance_criteria: "pm activity --limit N and the activity section of pm context parse only the history streams their window and requested rows can reach when valid derived stream bounds are present, so the cost is independent of the history archive outside those streams; a stream the window reaches is still parsed in full, and bounding the read inside a stream is the follow-up pm-kyf4z2; The bounds are derived, rebuildable and never authoritative: they are invalidated on append, replacement, repair, redaction and compaction, and a stale or missing summary falls back to the full scan with an explicit receipt field naming the fallback; pm activity and pm context output is identical with and without the summary on the same corpus, proven by a spec that includes redacted and compacted streams; scripts/bench gains a scale budget for a three-row pm context --for orient read on a fixture with thousands of history streams and at least 100 MB of history, with a declared p50 ceiling that CI fails on regression; Negative control: forcing the full-scan path on the benchmark fixture exceeds the declared ceiling"
parent: pm-9rxu
risk: medium
confidence: high
severity: medium
repro_steps: "On a tracker with thousands of history streams, time pm context --for orient --limit 3, pm context --limit 3 and pm activity --limit 3. The orient and activity reads scale with total history volume"
resolution: "Added runtime/history-stream-summary.json (src/core/history/stream-summary.ts): per-stream entry count and oldest/newest canonical timestamps keyed by the stat signature of the open file that supplied the bytes plus the reader's effective credentials, trusted only outside a two-second racy window, checksummed, never authoritative, engaged at 500 or more streams and bypassed under read hooks. runActivity skips trusted streams outside its window; pm context's activity section uses the new SDK readRecentActivity, which reads newest-first until no unread stream can outrank the cutoff. Unbounded reads disclose history_read / activity_history_read (summary_missing, summary_invalid, summary_stale, irregular_timestamps, read_hooks_active, unwindowed_query). scripts/bench/history-read-costs.mjs gates a three-row orient on 2,000 streams and 216 MiB in pnpm quality:static with a 0.6-of-full-scan fraction, a ratcheted declared ceiling and a full-scan negative control. Also parallelized stream existence probes and added the summary to pm gc --scope runtime."
expected_result: "A three-row recent-activity read costs time proportional to the streams its rows and window reach, with output identical to the complete read and an explicit receipt whenever it could not be bounded"
actual_result: "On this repository (3,354 streams, 135 MB) pm context --for orient --limit 3 fell from 4.96 s to 1.45 s and pm activity --limit 3 from 2.36 s to 0.90 s (pm list --limit 3 is 0.85 s); eight activity modes and orient are byte-identical to the released 2026.11.20 build under a fixed PM_CLOCK; parity specs cover redaction, compaction, restore, update, repair and raw appends; full coverage 100% across 13,503 tests"
affected_version: 2026.11.4
component: activity
