comments[29]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-11-20T10:27:38.283Z","harness:claude-code","Root cause, measured 2026-11-20 on this repository (3,354 streams, 135 MB): two archive-scale costs sat in the canonical cold-start read. (1) The context activity section called runActivity in compact mode, which has no lower time bound, so it parsed every stream, sorted every entry and then fingerprinted every matching row (stableStringify plus sha256, 1.6 s of a 4.96 s orient in a CPU profile) for a page cursor that context discards. (2) pm activity's default 24-hour digest parsed every stream to find the few entries inside its window. Fix: a derived runtime/history-stream-summary.json (src/core/history/stream-summary.ts) records per stream the entry count and oldest/newest canonical timestamps under its stat signature (dev, ino, size, mtimeNs, ctimeNs). An entry is trusted only while that signature is unchanged and the read that recorded it began more than two seconds after the stream's last change (Git's racy-clean rule), so every writer (append, redaction, compaction, repair, restore) invalidates it without cooperation; inode 0 and non-files never earn trust. runActivity skips trusted streams outside [from, to); context uses readRecentActivity, which reads untrusted and irregular streams first, then trusted regular streams in the activity order of their newest entry until the next one cannot outrank the last selected row (ties decided by item id). Read hooks keep the complete read (same rule as the metadata cache), and trackers under 500 streams never write the summary. Unbounded reads disclose history_read / activity_history_read with summary_missing, summary_invalid, summary_stale, read_hooks_active or unwindowed_query plus streams_read and streams_total. Results: orient 4.96 s -> 1.45 s, pm activity --limit 3 2.36 s -> 0.90 s (pm list --limit 3 is 0.85 s); pm activity in 8 modes and pm context orient produce byte-identical JSON to the released 2026.11.20 build under a fixed PM_CLOCK, receipts excluded."
  "2026-11-20T10:27:41.111Z","harness:claude-code","Evidence and scope notes. Specs: tests/unit/core/history/stream-summary.spec.ts (12: canonical timestamps, bounds, minimum, read hooks, missing/racy/append/same-size rename/stale, 14 malformed summary shapes, unwritable runtime directory, ino 0); tests/unit/commands/history/activity-bounded-reads.spec.ts (8: ten activity queries plus readRecentActivity equal the complete read on a corpus with a real history-redact stream, a real history-compact stream, old, tied, irregular and empty streams, and stay equal after append, redaction, compaction, restore, update, history-repair --salvage-tail and a raw append that moves an old stream into the window; a stream corrupted after its bounds were trusted raises the same history_json_invalid as the complete read). Benchmark: scripts/bench/history-read-costs.mjs (in pnpm quality:static) on 2,000 streams and 216 MiB measured bounded p50 1,045 ms vs full-scan p50 2,951 ms; the declared ceiling is 1,672 ms, the bounded p50 must stay under 0.6 of the same-runner full scan on every runner, and on a qualified runner the full scan must exceed the ceiling (negative control). The first read of a fresh fixture is excluded because it builds the SQLite history event index; that cold-start cost (about 6 s of a 20 s first orient on a fresh copy of this tracker) is filed as pm-s7z78x. Also parallelized enforceHistoryStreamPolicyForItems existence probes (130 -> 90 ms on 3,000 items) and added the summary to pm gc --scope runtime."
  "2026-11-20T10:59:40.889Z","harness:claude-code","Codex review round 1 (gpt-6.2-sol, xhigh, working tree): seven findings, six fixed. (1) The equal-timestamp branch of streamCanOutrank had no executions: a new spec makes four streams tie at the top-N cutoff, including an untrusted stream that fills the selection first while a tied stream with a smaller id must still be read, and unit-tests both comparators. (2) A damaged summary record with a matching signature could hide a non-empty stream: parseStoredStream now accepts only bounds summarizeHistoryStreamBounds can produce (empty means no timestamps and regular, a regular non-empty stream has both timestamps, timestamps appear together and in order). (3) One trusted stream suppressed the stale receipt after a bulk rewrite: summary_stale now applies once more than half of the streams lack current bounds. (4) A zero activity limit skipped every stream, so a damaged stream no longer raised its error: the untrusted and irregular streams are read for any limit, and only trusted regular streams are skipped. (5) A fractional SDK limit was passed raw to the collector, so the cutoff never filled: readRecentActivity passes the floored limit. (6) Distinct file names that collate equally (NFC vs NFD) could swap rows at the cutoff: file position is now the final tie-breaker in selection and stream ordering. Declined with reason: (7) a stream the window reaches is still parsed in full; bounding inside a stream needs an append-verified byte boundary (an in-place rewrite of equal or larger size defeats a size check), so it is filed as pm-kyf4z2 and the docs state the actual bound (the streams with entries in the window, not the archive)."
  "2026-11-20T11:16:10.206Z","harness:claude-code","Codex review round 2 (gpt-6.2-sol, xhigh): seven findings, all fixed. (1) A well-formed but damaged summary (a newest timestamp moved earlier) passed validation and record() never repaired it because the signature still matched: the file now carries a sha256 checksum of its streams payload and any mismatch makes it summary_invalid. (2) On a shared tracker another user could stat but not read a stream the first user's summary skips, hiding the complete read's EACCES: the reader's uid is part of every stream signature, so another reader's bounds are never trusted. (3) The newest-N loop re-sorted the whole selection after every stream, quadratic for a large --activity-limit: rows now accumulate unsorted and the pool is cut back to the limit only once it doubles; between cuts the stop check uses the last cut's row, which ranks no higher than the true cutoff, so stopping stays sound. (4) bench --update could raise the committed ceiling: it now keeps the committed ceiling unless --allow-increase <reviewed reason> is given (the regenerated ceiling ratcheted from 1,672 to 1,652 ms). (5) A tracker whose current bounds are mostly irregular pruned nothing yet emitted no receipt: a new irregular_timestamps reason covers it. (6) The negative control compared the full scan with the nominal ceiling while the bounded check adds a 100 ms margin: both now use the enforced ceiling. (7) enforceHistoryStreamPolicyForItems re-probes each missing stream before creating it, so a stream created meanwhile (by another writer or an earlier creation's write hook) is neither reported as auto-created nor passed to a write hook; tests/unit/core/history/history-stream-policy.spec.ts covers both and the chunk boundary."
  "2026-11-20T11:30:54.571Z","harness:claude-code","Codex review round 3 (gpt-6.2-sol, xhigh): seven findings, all fixed. (1) A stream that vanished between the stat and the read (directory moved aside and restored) was recorded as empty under its unchanged signature: activity now reads through readHistorySnapshot and record() keeps bounds only when the byte length read equals the observed size. (2) Every Windows account shared the reader identity '-': the reader is now the OS account (uid on POSIX, account name where there is none) from os.userInfo, and no stream earns trust when neither is available. (3) The file-position map held every discarded candidate and its patch payload: it uses weak keys, and the pool is cut back during the first pass as well, so a three-row read retains rows proportional to its limit. (4) Half stale plus half irregular crossed neither threshold: stale and irregular streams count together and the larger cause names the receipt. (5) A write hook that removes a later stream after the bulk probe left it missing under auto_create: with write hooks active every id is probed sequentially right before its turn (hasActiveOnWriteHooks), as before this change. (6) The benchmark child inherited a caller's PM_CLOCK/PM_SEED, which could empty the activity window: it drops PM_CLOCK, PM_CLOCK_TICK_MS and PM_SEED. (7) The packaged report linked into .agents/pm: it uses the canonical GitHub URL labelled repository-only. Separately the absolute ceiling headroom is 2.0x (reset once through --allow-increase with a recorded reason, now 1,984 ms) because the min() ratchet followed the fastest local run; the runner-independent 0.6 fraction gate stays primary."
  "2026-11-20T11:45:05.571Z","harness:claude-code","Codex review round 4 (gpt-6.2-sol, xhigh): six findings. Rounds 3 and 4 each found another way the path and the bytes can diverge (a vanished stream, then an equal-sized swap during a restore), so instead of another path check the record is now anchored on the open file: activity reads through readIdentifiedHistorySnapshot (src/core/history/read.ts), which fstats the handle that supplies the bytes, and record() stores bounds under that handle's signature and only when the bytes are the whole of that file; no path change can bind bounds to a revision that did not supply them. Also fixed: (2) the negative control could fail a faster runner whose full scan stays under the ceiling; in --check the absolute ceiling is now enforced only where it discriminates (qualified runner and full scan above the enforced ceiling) and otherwise reported unverified with a reason (runner_unqualified or full_scan_within_ceiling), while --update still refuses a ceiling its own measurement cannot prove; (3) sessions of one uid with different supplementary groups shared trust: the reader identity is uid, gid and sorted groups on POSIX; (5) a non-string recorded agent_harness crashed runActivity's provenance matcher with unknown_error (pre-existing, also in 2026.11.20), filed and fixed as pm-sew1dj so raw, recent and provenance reads all return the row; (6) the CLI spec now pins PM_PATH and PM_GLOBAL_PATH inside its fixture. Declined with reason: (4) owner-only permissions for the summary: it discloses strictly less than the item metadata cache (runtime/metadata-cache.json, titles and bodies) and the history streams themselves, which pm writes with the default umask, so restricting this one derived file would not change what a co-tenant can read; tracker-wide runtime permissions would be their own change."
  "2026-11-20T12:03:24.417Z","harness:claude-code","Codex review round 5 (gpt-6.2-sol, xhigh): five findings, four fixed. (2) getuid/getgid are real ids and the identity was cached for the process: the reader identity is now the effective uid, effective gid and current supplementary groups, computed once per summary session, so a long-lived SDK host that drops privileges never reuses bounds. (3) Disabled sessions (under 500 streams, read hooks active) paid an extra handle stat per stream: they read through the plain readHistorySnapshot. (4) A path-validation error in the bulk existence probe preempted an earlier sorted id's refusal or creation: such an id stays a candidate and raises at its own turn. (5) commit() could recreate a tracker removed during the read through writeFileAtomic's recursive parent creation: it now creates only runtime/ beneath an existing tracker and writes without parent creation. Declined with reason: (1) re-validating every skipped stream after the read. A complete read is not an atomic snapshot either (docs already state continuation keeps no atomic cross-file snapshot): a stream that changes while either read runs may or may not be reflected, depending on whether the change lands before that stream is reached. A second bulk stat of every skipped stream would add tens of milliseconds to every bounded read to narrow, not close, that window; docs/COMMANDS.md now says so explicitly."
  "2026-11-20T12:12:15.186Z","harness:claude-code","Codex review round 6 (gpt-6.2-sol, xhigh): four findings, two fixed. (3) pm activity --json --stream built its own meta record and dropped history_read: the meta record now carries it. (4) unwindowed_query fired whenever --from was absent, even when a --to before most of the archive pruned every stream (streams_read 0): it now applies only when the read parsed most streams, the same majority rule as the stale receipt. Declined with reason and filed as pm-4zqpr3: (1) attribute-caching network mounts and (2) Windows tokens of one account sharing trust. Both are properties of every stat-keyed derived cache, the item metadata cache first (ADR pm-vnie), so the summary copies an accepted trust model rather than introducing one; a fix belongs in one tracker-wide policy (statfs-detected network filesystems or a setting, token elevation on Windows). docs/COMMANDS.md now states that limit instead of claiming credential isolation on Windows."
  "2026-11-20T12:52:31.749Z","harness:claude-code","Codex review round 7 (gpt-6.2-sol, xhigh, PR head vs main): three findings, all fixed in the follow-up commit. (1) Owner-only summary permissions, raised again after the round-4 decline: implemented rather than re-declined, since it is cheap; writeFileAtomic gained an optional mode, and the summary and its temporary file are created 0600. (2) With current bounds, a recent read under a clock pinned between most streams' oldest and newest entries parsed every stream without a receipt: an engaged read that parses most streams now always discloses it, as unwindowed_query without a lower bound or window_reaches_most_streams otherwise. (3) A FIFO at the summary path could stall the read: the summary is read through the existing readRegularFile (O_NOFOLLOW, O_NONBLOCK, regular files only), and anything else is summary_invalid. Specs: a real mkfifo case and the 0600 mode on POSIX, and the straddling-stream pinned-clock case."
  "2026-11-20T12:59:31.374Z","harness:claude-code","CodeRabbit full review of ac1eaea87: two findings, both fixed. (1, minor) Re-check trusted bounds before the read completes. This reverses my round-5 decline of the same Codex point. CodeRabbit's example shows a result no complete read can produce: stream b is appended (13:00) and then stream a (12:00), both after the session opened. The recent read takes a/12:00 and skips b on its stale 10:00 bound, but a complete read in file order that sees a's later append must also see b's earlier one. Now, before either read path finishes, it re-stats the streams it skipped on trusted bounds (session.revalidate) and reads every one whose signature changed. The window path merges their rows back in history-file order; in the recent path a better cutoff can only exclude more. A change that lands after that check is one a complete read could also miss. The cost is one stat pass over the skipped streams, about 100 ms on this repository. The benchmark ceiling was reset once with --allow-increase for this deliberate cost: 2,280 ms, with bounded p50 1,140 ms against a full scan of 3,578 ms. (2, trivial) The write-path spec now asserts, per mutation, that a fresh session no longer trusts the affected stream and that the next digest re-reads exactly that file, instead of only checking for a nonzero read count."
  "2026-11-20T13:06:43.056Z","harness:claude-code","Codex review round 8 (gpt-6.2-sol, xhigh, on the CodeRabbit and round-7 fixes): four findings, all fixed. (1) The SDK surface snapshot was stale after the new receipt reason and writeFileAtomic's mode option: regenerated from main's snapshot with one acknowledgement entry. contract_change now names writeFileAtomic. (2) The owner-only claim does not hold on Windows, where Node's mode sets no ACL: docs and the code comment now limit it to POSIX (0600), and on Windows the file inherits the runtime directory's ACL. (3) A bulk rewrite of most skipped streams during the read was reported as window_reaches_most_streams: when revalidation forced most of the reads, the receipt now says summary_stale. A spec rewrites sixteen archive streams mid-read. (4) The docs promised that a completed change is never dropped: they now describe a best-effort per-stream recheck of skipped streams, and say that like a complete read the bounded read is not an atomic snapshot."
  "2026-11-20T13:55:40.511Z","harness:claude-code","Codex review round 9 (gpt-6.2-sol, xhigh, PR head 1b1bd929c): five findings, four fixed, one filed. (1) Another account able to write runtime/ could author a checksummed summary: an opened summary is now trusted only when it is an unshared regular file (nlink 1) of plausible size and, on POSIX, owned by the effective uid and not group- or other-writable. (2) A redirected runtime/ (symlink or junction) let reads and writes escape the tracker: both now require lstat to report a real directory, the rule the context-usage ledger already applies; the summary is opened O_NOFOLLOW | O_NONBLOCK. (3) The benchmark ceiling had been measured on Node 26 while docs/PERFORMANCE.md pins baselines to the release major: --update now refuses a report from any major other than RELEASE_NODE_MAJOR (24, and a spec checks it against release.yml), and the budget was recalibrated on Node 24.22.3: ceiling 2,246 ms, bounded p50 1,123 ms against a full scan of 3,385 ms. (4) Stale-at-open and stale-mid-read counts are disjoint and now add up before the receipt picks summary_stale. (5) Filed as pm-cqddvl rather than fixed here: pm gc --scope runtime removing through a redirected runtime directory, which predates this change and applies to every runtime target."
  "2026-11-20T14:25:31.362Z","harness:claude-code","Codex review round 10 (gpt-6.2-sol, xhigh, round-9 fixes plus the pm-55jxhy tranche): five findings, all fixed. (1) Windows has no O_NOFOLLOW, so a symlinked summary file to a regular file could be read: the summary entry is lstat-checked before opening, as the context-usage ledger does. (2) The size cap rejected valid summaries for accounts with many supplementary groups, because every signature repeated the full group list (769,994 B against a 516,096 B cap for 500 streams and 125 groups): the reader identity in each signature is now a fixed 16-hex sha256 digest. (3) A file grown after its stat could still be read past the cap: readAtMost reads at most cap + 1 bytes and refuses an over-limit result. (4) bench --update built the 216 MiB fixture before refusing a non-24 Node major: the runtime major is checked first. (5) A canonical REPOSITORY_TESTING link in TESTING.md lacked its repository-only note."
  "2026-11-20T14:31:23.467Z","harness:claude-code","CodeRabbit review of 1b1bd929c: four minor findings, all fixed. (1) pm-sew1dj's text claimed every malformed harness is unresolved; corrected through pm, since a vocabulary alias still resolves. (2) With no write hooks, a stream the bulk probe saw present but another process removed while missing streams were created escaped the policy: when the probe found anything missing, the streams it saw present are re-probed in order afterwards. With nothing missing there is no re-probe, so the fast path is kept, and a spec covers the removal. (3) The stale receipt compared stale rereads with every listed stream: it now compares them with the streams actually parsed, so 50 stale rereads in a 51-stream read report summary_stale rather than window_reaches_most_streams. (4) readRecentActivity's JSDoc now says the limit caps rows, not history parsed, and that a tracker under 500 streams parses every stream with no receipt."
  "2026-11-20T14:43:44.683Z","harness:claude-code","Codex review round 11 (gpt-6.2-sol, xhigh, PR head 5fd058140): three findings, no new defect. All three repeat scope already filed with recorded reasons: (1) stale attributes on attribute-caching network mounts and (2) unverifiable summary permissions on Windows are both pm-4zqpr3, one tracker-wide policy for every stat-keyed cache, the metadata cache first, and docs/COMMANDS.md states both limits; (3) pm gc following a redirected runtime directory is pm-cqddvl, which predates this change and applies to every runtime target. The local review loop has converged, with no new correctness finding in this round."
  "2026-11-20T15:10:47.501Z","harness:claude-code","CodeRabbit review of 5fd058140: five findings, all fixed. (1) The acceptance criteria still promised a read independent of total history volume, while pm-kyf4z2 records that a reached stream is parsed in full. The criteria now state the delivered scope: only the streams the window and rows can reach are parsed, and bounding inside a stream is that follow-up. The issue stays closed on that reviewed scope. (2) A focused-run example in docs/REPOSITORY_TESTING.md, moved unchanged from TESTING.md, named a spec path that no longer exists; it now names tests/unit/core/output/output.spec.ts. (3) Vocabulary alias lookup used the object's prototype, so an author named constructor resolved to a function: only own configured string aliases count. (4) The stream-policy recheck spec could remove pm-c during the bulk probe; it now removes it on pm-a's second probe, and a negative control confirmed that the spec fails without the recheck. (5) Bench policy specs no longer depend on the calibrated committed ceiling; a separate spec checks the committed budget with values derived from its own ceiling."
  "2026-11-20T15:23:23.315Z","harness:claude-code","Codex review round 12 (gpt-6.2-sol, xhigh, PR head 4f5e8801f): four findings, two fixed and two declined with reasons. Fixed: (3) a read whose parses were mostly irregular streams reported window_reaches_most_streams; streams read because their trusted bounds are irregular are now counted, and stale plus irregular reads together pick the reason, naming the larger cause. (4) the benchmark generator ran PmClient.init under the caller's global profile; it now runs inside withFixtureGlobalProfile. Declined: (1) a scratch root inside a Git checkout is a property of the shared generateSyntheticWorkspace, used by every benchmark, and is filed as pm-fh07lz with prior art pm-bukwmy. (2) A runtime/ directory swapped for a link between the check and the write is the concurrent-replacement case that docs/BOUNDED_CONTEXT_READS.md already documents for the context-usage ledger: portable Node APIs cannot pin ancestor directories. docs/COMMANDS.md now states the same limit for the summary."
  "2026-11-20T16:00:23.373Z","harness:claude-code","Codex review round 13 (gpt-6.2-sol, xhigh, PR head 97d09f0ab): three findings, two fixed. (2) The full-scan control removed the summary before each sample, so every control sample also rebuilt and wrote it, which bounded samples never pay. The control now puts a directory at the summary path: the read is summary_invalid, parses every stream and persists nothing. Recalibrated on Node 24.22.3: bounded p50 720 ms against a full scan of 2,070 ms, and the ratchet lowered the ceiling to 1,440 ms. The hosted static gate on 4f5e8801f measured 683 ms against 2,035 ms, so the enforced 1,540 ms ceiling now discriminates on hosted runners as well, where the old 2,246 ms ceiling left the check unverified there. (3) --allow-increase reduced its reason to a boolean; blank reasons are refused, and a real increase records last_increase { reason, from_ms, to_ms, measured_on } in the budget, carried forward by later updates. Declined: (1) Windows summary ownership, a repeat of pm-4zqpr3."
  "2026-11-20T16:13:01.849Z","harness:claude-code","CodeRabbit review of 97d09f0ab: six findings, all fixed. (1) pm-4zqpr3's criteria counted only elevation; they now require an access-equivalent Windows identity (enabled groups and restricted SIDs too). (2) The docs-only checklist in docs/REPOSITORY_TESTING.md now includes pnpm quality:docs-links. (3) docs/SDK.md now says readRecentActivity's limit caps returned rows, not streams parsed, and that read hooks or fewer than 500 streams mean a full read. (4) pm gc following a redirected runtime directory is fixed in this PR rather than deferred: every runtime-scope target and the snapshot temp sweep are retained, with a warning, when runtime/ is not a real directory, and pm-cqddvl is closed. (5) The racy-window spec no longer depends on scheduling: it keeps the real clock and records a source whose change time is after the read began. (6) The benchmark passes an explicit phase (warmup, bounded, full_scan) to measure, and its spec stub reads the phase instead of counting calls."
  "2026-11-20T16:28:17.110Z","harness:claude-code","Codex review round 14 (gpt-6.2-sol, xhigh, PR head 1156d398d): three findings. (3) Fixed: markdown context dropped activity_history_read; the Recent activity section now ends with a 'history read: <reason>, <read>/<total> streams parsed' line, and prints it even when no rows match. (1) The control still computed bounds before the blocked write failed. Measured on the fixture, bounds computation was 256 ms per 200,000 entries, about 12% of the full-scan p50, and almost all of it was the Date.parse plus toISOString round trip per entry. isCanonicalHistoryTimestamp now checks the calendar fields directly (month, days in month with leap years, hour, minute, second), and a property spec proves it equals the round trip over every calendar edge and 5,000 seeded random field sets. Bounds now cost 104 ms and serialization 5 ms, so the control's remaining non-traversal work is a few percent, and every real summary rebuild got cheaper. The full-scan control otherwise stays the read a user gets when the summary cannot be used. (2) Declined, a repeat: benchmark scratch roots inside a Git worktree are pm-fh07lz."
  "2026-11-20T17:10:05.118Z","harness:claude-code","Codex review round 15 (gpt-6.2-sol, xhigh, PR head 80d9da125): four findings, two fixed. (3) The full-scan control still paid for a stat pass and per-stream bounds before its blocked write failed. Fixed at the source rather than in the bench: when no commit could ever persist the summary (runtime/ exists but is not a real directory, or a directory occupies the summary path), openHistoryStreamSummary returns a disabled session with summary_invalid, so the read is the complete read with no summary work. The read and commit keep their own redirect checks, and a spec redirects runtime/ after the writability check to prove both still refuse. The bench control now times exactly the read a user gets when the summary is unusable; recalibrated on Node 24.22.3 (bounded p50 843 ms against a 2,227 ms full scan, ceiling kept at 1,440 ms by the ratchet). (4) Under strict_error the bulk existence probe visited every stream before refusing the first missing one; it now stops after the first chunk of 64 that holds a candidate, and the existing in-order recheck covers every id it never reached, so a stream created meanwhile still lets a later missing stream be refused (both proven by specs). Declined, repeats of pm-4zqpr3: (1) NFS attribute caching and (2) Windows token equivalence for stat-keyed trust."
  "2026-11-20T17:17:43.540Z","harness:claude-code","CodeRabbit review of 80d9da125: one finding, fixed. summarizeHistoryStreamBounds read entry.ts on a null entry, because the decoder accepts any JSON value per line. Non-object entries now count as irregular, so the summary never adds a crash of its own. The released 2026.11.20 build already crashes on such a line in pm activity and pm context (unknown_error, \"Cannot read properties of null (reading 'ts')\"), and so does this branch. A 520-stream comparison confirms both fail identically with the summary engaged. The typed refusal, and a recovery that works given that salvage-tail refuses non-object lines, are filed as pm-jbplbq."
  "2026-11-20T17:34:26.774Z","harness:claude-code","Codex review round 16 (gpt-6.2-sol, xhigh, PR head 4e55d3e49): three findings, two fixed. (2) A FIFO, socket or link at the summary path was refused as input but could still be replaced by a commit. Any existing entry at the summary path that is not a regular file now makes the summary unwritable: the session is disabled with summary_invalid, no bounds are computed, and the entry is left alone. The commit also re-checks the destination just before replacing it, and specs cover a link swapped in during the read, a FIFO, a hard-linked summary and a link reported between the writability check and the read. (3) writeFileAtomic left a partly written temporary file when the write itself failed (ENOSPC, EDQUOT). The write is now inside the cleanup path, and the repeated best-effort unlinks share one helper. (1) Declined into pm-4zqpr3: a Landlock or LSM sandbox can allow stat but deny open without changing credentials, so access equivalence cannot be judged from credentials on any platform. pm-4zqpr3's criteria now cover that case."
  "2026-11-20T17:51:15.319Z","harness:claude-code","Package headroom after Codex round 16: the review fixes took the clean local base distribution to 20,000,278 B, 278 B over budget. The pm-55jxhy tranche was extended with docs/generated/FLAG_LEXICON_BUDGETS.md; measured at 19,996,083 B. The structural census for the next tranche is recorded on pm-998juj."
  "2026-11-20T18:22:21.816Z","harness:claude-code","Codex review round 17 (gpt-6.2-sol, xhigh, PR head e27e5636b): one finding, fixed. gc now re-checks runtime/ after each target's read hook and again before the snapshot temp sweep (details on pm-cqddvl)."
  "2026-11-20T18:34:34.907Z","harness:claude-code","CodeRabbit review of e27e5636b: five findings, all fixed. (1) gate_evidence.negative_control described the superseded control; it now records the directory-based control (summary_invalid, nothing computed or persisted), the declared 1,440 ms ceiling enforced at 1,540 ms with the 100 ms noise margin, and this control's Node 24 measurements. (2) SDK.md and COMMANDS.md now give both conditions for summary_stale and irregular_timestamps: more than half of all streams at open, or, when the read parsed most streams, more than half of those it parsed. (3) A read hook registered while a bounded read runs ends pruning: at revalidation every skipped stream is read, so the hook observes every stream once and none is replayed, and the receipt reports read_hooks_active. A spec registers the hook from inside the first stream read in both read paths. (4) streams_read counted streams removed between the listing and their read; it now counts only streams whose bytes existed. Both specs fail with the fix disabled. (5) The hook-predicate spec now covers read-only and write-only registries. Separately, the Gates (static) failure on e27e5636b was tracker-context-quality: pm-jbplbq lacked risk, confidence and an estimate (now medium, 80 and 180 minutes)."
  "2026-11-20T18:55:23.419Z","harness:claude-code","Codex review round 18 (gpt-6.2-sol, xhigh, PR head 7b67da53f): three findings, all fixed. (1) A read hook registered while revalidate() awaited its stats was missed, because the hook check ran before the await. It now runs again after the stats, so the hook observes every skipped stream, once each, and the receipt reports read_hooks_active. A spec registers the hook from inside revalidate in both read paths. (2) When the bulk probe found a missing stream, verification visited the candidates before the streams found present. An earlier-sorting stream removed after the bulk probe was therefore handled after a later one: strict mode could refuse the wrong stream, and auto_create reported repairs out of order. Once anything is missing, every id is now verified in sorted order; with nothing missing the fast path is unchanged. (3) A missing or misspelled threshold in scripts/bench/history-read-budgets.json compared as NaN and silently disabled the fraction gate. validateHistoryReadBudgets refuses such a budget in --check, and in --update for a committed file, where a lost previous ceiling would bypass the ratchet. Specs 1 and 2 fail with their fix disabled."
  "2026-11-20T19:31:52.685Z","harness:claude-code","Codex review round 19 (gpt-6.2-sol, xhigh, PR head b5ff34da1): four findings, three fixed. (3) Regression from round 18: the hook check after revalidation ignored whether the session could skip at all, so a tracker under 500 streams with read hooks active gained a read_hooks_active receipt. The checks now apply only to sessions that skip streams, and a spec proves small-tracker reads carry no receipt. (2) A hook registered while a stale skipped stream was re-read missed the remaining unchanged skipped streams. Both collectors now share readSkippedStreams, which checks for hooks after every await: before revalidation, after it, and after stale-stream recovery. When hooks appear, every skipped stream not yet read is read, so after the last check none is left unread. A spec registers the hook during the stale re-read in both paths, and both new specs fail with their fix disabled. (4) --update treated any budget read failure as a first calibration; only ENOENT counts as absence now, so an unreadable budget cannot drop the ratchet's ceiling. (1) Declined, a repeat of pm-4zqpr3: Windows summary trust without owner or ACL verification."
  "2026-11-20T19:44:12.487Z","harness:claude-code","Gates (static) on b5ff34da1 failed the token budget gate: activity-default measured 757 estimated tokens against its 736-token budget, which has zero headroom. Cause: the round-18 regression that Codex round 19 and CodeRabbit both reported. The token corpus has read hooks active on a small tracker, so the post-revalidation hook check emitted a read_hooks_active receipt. Restoring the regression locally reproduces the identical 757-token failure, and the round-19 fix passes the gate, so the token gate acted as an independent negative control."
